{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread","id":1535008,"title":"Ancient Setite metaplot thread","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-05-01T07:55:02-07:00","id":1535008,"title":"Ancient Setite metaplot thread","content":"Hello there,<br /> <br /> I'm opening this thread to welcome suggestions to retcon the Followers of Set's metaplot to explain the origins and deeds of the two bloodlines introduced in VDA20 and practically extinct by 1244/V20: Witches of Echidna (thought extinct by 1244 but resurfacing among the Anarchs in V20/V5) and Warriors of Glycon (who went extinct at an unspecified point in time between 1244 and V20).<br /> <br /> The lore about them is basically entirely found in VDA20 core manual and VDA20 Tome of Secrets, and it seems like the Witches of Echidna were well respected among the Mediterranean clans in the ancient world as priestesses, seers, spiritual or religious advisors, mystics and sorceresses. They gradually declined with the rise of Christianity first and the rise of the Tremere later.<br /> The Warriors of Glycon seem to have been the leaders and rulers of the clan until the priest bloodline got their own warriors and plotted to wrestle control of the clan away from them, which they succeeded at between 1244 and V20.<br /> The clan also has a much more international stock and presence in VDA20 than in previous editions: only the priests seem to have strong ties to Egypt (and more likely Hellenistic Egypt than previous eras): the Witches are mentioned as centered in Greece, but found more or less anywhere (even France and Germany and further North, or in Costantinople and surrounding areas), while the Warriors of Glycon are mentioned as being in North Africa, but Glycon hitorically was a deity originating from Macedonian snake-cults and a city in today's Turkey. Echidna, Typhon and Glycon all have strong historical and mythological links to Greece, Asia Minor and Macedonia, and this portion of Greek myths is often aptly compared to Mesopotamian myths around Tiamat and other snake-themed monsters or deities and early battles between gods and monsters or titans, as if the Greeks were influenced by these older myths early on in their religious history.<br /> <br /> This is, more or less, the data, and more can be found in the manuals. I'm eager to hear your ideas to flesh out more the early history of the clan and these bloodlines. I'm currently playing a French Witch of Echidna, after having played two Warriors of Glycon.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T08:54:35-07:00","id":1535015,"title":"","content":"Well this is just my headcanon, so anyting I wrote treat it wit ha grain of salt!<br /> <br /> I think the best is to trace their origin to the Second Baali War<br /> Around 1500 BC the power of the Baali was broken when almostt all the Clans allied against them.Legendary Cainites from across the Middle East, Europe, Africa, and central Asia united against &quot;Shaitan&quot;. Among them were the Brujah Menele, the Malkavian known only as the Dionysian, the Ventrue Balthazar, <b>and Setite sorcerers led directly by their Antediluvian himself!</b><br /> Sutekh led an elite group of Setite sorcerers in assisting in the defeat of Nergal at Knossos, which ends with the eruption of Thera, leaving behind the Santorini Caldera and basically rewrote the geological and geopolitical landscape<br /> <br /> While the Dark God went home, it is logical to assume, he left behind some sorceres to keep an eye on the clean up, rooting out any lingering demonic presence and establish a foothold in the area - this would explain the Witches, who branched out from these sorceres, embracing the ancient art of divination<br /> <br /> The Warriors of Glycon is a bit complicated, since cult of the snake god Glykon was introduced in in the mid-second century CE, so it is a bit of a historical hickup, or they renamed themselves...<br /> The Warrior Setites claim descent from the methuselah Wepwawet, worshiped as a war deity in ancient Egypt and who is a quite ancient diety, he is depicted on the Narmer Palette witch is dated back 3150 BC. <br /> The Warrior Setites are a specialized branch of the main Clan that came into existence allegedly <i>after</i> the end of the Warriors of Glycon <br /> The math don't really add up to be honest, it is more likely, the Warriors of Glycon are a splinter group of the Warrior Setites<br /> <br /> It would explain actually, those Sorceres came with their warrior bodyguards with Sutekh, then a handfull of them stayed behind and developed a Lamia-Cappadocian like symbiotic relationship - it is even possible they &quot;borrowed&quot; the idea, since the Graverobbers were a prominent presence in that area","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T10:24:11-07:00","id":1535028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535015#post1535015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well this is just my headcanon, so anyting I wrote treat it wit ha grain of salt!<br /> <br /> I think the best is to trace their origin to the Second Baali War<br /> Around 1500 BC the power of the Baali was broken when almostt all the Clans allied against them.Legendary Cainites from across the Middle East, Europe, Africa, and central Asia united against &quot;Shaitan&quot;. Among them were the Brujah Menele, the Malkavian known only as the Dionysian, the Ventrue Balthazar, <b>and Setite sorcerers led directly by their Antediluvian himself!</b><br /> Sutekh led an elite group of Setite sorcerers in assisting in the defeat of Nergal at Knossos, which ends with the eruption of Thera, leaving behind the Santorini Caldera and basically rewrote the geological and geopolitical landscape<br /> <br /> While the Dark God went home, it is logical to assume, he left behind some sorceres to keep an eye on the clean up, rooting out any lingering demonic presence and establish a foothold in the area - this would explain the Witches, who branched out from these sorceres, embracing the ancient art of divination<br /> <br /> The Warriors of Glycon is a bit complicated, since cult of the snake god Glykon was introduced in in the mid-second century CE, so it is a bit of a historical hickup, or they renamed themselves...<br /> The Warrior Setites claim descent from the methuselah Wepwawet, worshiped as a war deity in ancient Egypt and who is a quite ancient diety, he is depicted on the Narmer Palette witch is dated back 3150 BC.<br /> The Warrior Setites are a specialized branch of the main Clan that came into existence allegedly <i>after</i> the end of the Warriors of Glycon<br /> The math don't really add up to be honest, it is more likely, the Warriors of Glycon are a splinter group of the Warrior Setites<br /> <br /> It would explain actually, those Sorceres came with their warrior bodyguards with Sutekh, then a handfull of them stayed behind and developed a Lamia-Cappadocian like symbiotic relationship - it is even possible they &quot;borrowed&quot; the idea, since the Graverobbers were a prominent presence in that area</div> </div> </div> </div>As for it being headcanon, that is exactly what \u0130 want to hear, everyone's headcanon! Thank you!<br /> <br /> \u0130 love the idea of the Baali wars and Sutekh bringing his own sorcerers etc. \u0130 think it has plenty of potential and more stuff could be extracted from that. For example, maybe Sutekh was affected by some Baali stuff, and that is why the Egyptian line became gradually more fanatical and ended up persecuting the other Setite bloodlines and isolating itself by the 13th century.<br /> <br /> As for the warriors, you're spot on with regards to the historical cult of Glycon: in my headcanon, either the Warriors of Glycon rename themselves at some point after the 2nd century CE, or the historical cult is due to some member of the bloodline or ghoul becoming a bit too famous, while the name itself was much older but only known among vampires.<br /> <br /> As for the Warrior Setites vs Warriors of Glycon: the timeline is not wrong: VDA20 core manual clearly states the priests <i>already had their personal guard trained in Potence</i> while the Warriors of Glycon still ruled the clan, and this was one of the reasons why there was fear that they may do them dirty, as the witches had been prophesising for a while, but the Warriors of Glycon did not listen. The difference is precisely that the Glycon guys (WoG from now on) ruled the clan, while the priests handled the temples and preaching and the witches (WoE) handled the rituals and mysticism, and the priests made their own sub-bloodline of Warrior Setites as bodyguards by training them hard as ghouls, maybe because not WoG had reason to lower himself to be a priest's bodyguard (my speculation). <br /> As for Wepwawet, in some V20 manuals it is mentioned that nobody has actually seen Wepwawet, and there is speculation he may be a lie spun by Warrior Setites to give more legitimacy to their line and hunt down dissidents and heretics. So it's not a given that he exists. <br /> <br /> \u0130t's also worth mentioning that the Witches of Echidna - who prophesised their own being hunt down centuries in advance - in VDA20 core manual describe the Egyptian priests' fanaticism about Set/Sutekh as a weird form of zealotry originating from Hellenistic Egypt. Both the WoG and the WoE write up in VDA20 and the details added in Tome of Secrets make a good case for the Egyptian form of the clan as being later, while the Greece/Asia Minor form based on Typhon and Echidna should be earlier.<br /> <br /> Hellenistic Egypt's syncretism might be the culprit for Egyptian myths entering the clan, and attempts may have been made to make Setism look more ancient as a religion by banking on the reputation of Egyptian myths and rituals as being older than Greece's own. This is my speculation.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T11:20:13-07:00","id":1535030,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535028#post1535028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u0130 love the idea of the Baali wars and Sutekh bringing his own sorcerers etc.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>It is actually a canon &quot;fact&quot;, it is in the 1997 Dark Ages Companion, p. 171 <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I love to build my headcanons on quasy historical facts<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535028#post1535028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> in VDA20 core manual describe the Egyptian priests' fanaticism about Set/Sutekh as a weird form of zealotry originating from Hellenistic Egypt. Both the WoG and the WoE write up in VDA20 and the details added in Tome of Secrets make a good case for the Egyptian form of the clan as being later, while the Greece/Asia Minor form based on Typhon and Echidna should be earlier.<br /> <br /> Hellenistic Egypt's syncretism might be the culprit for Egyptian myths entering the clan, and attempts may have been made to make Setism look more ancient as a religion by banking on the reputation of Egyptian myths and rituals as being older than Greece's own. This is my speculation.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that is the WoG and the WoE point of view or a heavy retcon<br /> Since other - older of course- material state around 13,000 BC the Settites appeared in Africa and both of Cairo by Night and Rage Across Egypt details and event called rhe Week of the Midnight Sun - it was a supernatural event that occurred in Egypt during the pre-Dynastic period, when Sakhmet, a legendary Simba warrior queen struck against those who dare oppose the light and embrace the dark god Set. Sakhmet's bond with the Celestine Ra was so great that her powers allowed her to force a red sun shine from the Umbra even in the night. The Week of the Midnight Sun was intended to end the dominion of Set. Sakhmet slaughtered the herds of the Setites, spread vile diseases, and fought several of his childer, among them the Scorpion King who she managed to destroy.\u200b The event drove the Setites into hiding, allowing the followers of Horus to conquer Upper Egypt and unite the Two Lands. (and secured a special place for the Bastet in the dark heart of Followers; the Bubasti helping Horus not helped them and they paid the price)<br /> The pre-Dynastic period was around 5000\u20133000 BC<br /> The oldest civilization in greece was the Minoan civilization, it was located on the island of Crete and existed from approximately 2700 BCE to 1450 BCE<br /> So the Followers and the veneration of Sutekh is deffinitly more older than their greece counterpart\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T12:15:59-07:00","id":1535032,"title":"","content":"I really like this thread. I find Setite history fascinating and have a special interest in the role of the Followers of Set in the Ptolemaic Egypt ruled by Cleopatra in the time of the late Roman Republic. In fact, in a V20 chronicle I played in a couple years ago I had a Greek Toreador PC who flirted heavily with Setite beliefs and also became involved with a Ventrue plot to install Julius Caesar as a Roman dictator.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":26996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","user":"Amethyst","date":". 05-01-2024, 05:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T13:21:24-07:00","id":1535036,"title":"","content":"There has been so many retcons and changes to the Setites over the years that it's hard to reconcile everything. The Setites were established with some very specific lore that got in the way of viewing them as just another clan, so Revised made a lot of changes to them and V5 did even more. A lot of the uniqueness of the Setites were thus lost. So you'll need to decide what to do when trying to incorporate canon into your own ideas because canon can be contradictory. I tend to default to the earlier canon myself when these things come up.<br /> <br /> If you go by the original description of the clan, it's important to note that around 900 BC, Set was defeated in Egypt, driven into exile, and his followers slaughtered. At that point, Set reviewed what had gone wrong and decided to make changes. The Followers of Set, as a clan with a culture and organization that we recognize today, is a result of that. In other words, the Setites were different prior to 900 BC. They were probably more like other clans, and the temple/cell based structure or corrupting things behind the scenes was adopted. It isn't clear whether all other Setites were destroyed by this time, though I think it's implied to be the case. It is also unclear whether all the other Setites were destroyed by Set's enemies, or whether Set &quot;cleaned house&quot; by eliminating any remaining Setites that he didn't find up to snuff. I imagine there could have been some Setites who survived the attacks by their enemies and were not purged by Set.<br /> <br /> So if I was going to include any Setite bloodlines that existed during the Dark Ages and/or today, I would only have them be created after 900 BC. Another option would be to link one or both bloodlines to the previous generation of Setites before 900 BC. If so, that bloodline might be seen as heretical since Set did not want them to survive. They represent the previous mistakes that caused his earlier failure. That would explain why that bloodline(s) later went extinct &quot;again&quot; - the other Setites wiped them out since they are not part of Set's master plan. Obviously, using this option would create a very different dynamic for characters who are of this bloodline. It would also explain why this bloodline is now part of the Anarchs rather than aligned with the actual Clan.<br /> <br /> Another thing I would probably do is trace any new characters of such bloodlines to a single survivor recently awakened from torpor. The bloodlines did seem to go extinct, but since all it takes is one survivor to create new ones, it's easy to come back. A single character in long term torpor who woke up is an easy explanation of what happened between then and now. Put your creative energy into making that single survivor interesting and how that character is viewed and interacted with by its relatively few descendants.<br /> <br /> Personally, I have never liked the idea of sub-clans - those bloodlines that are members of the actual clan but had different in-clan Disciplines or different clan weaknesses. I just make them all mechanically the same. It's not like vampires cannot learn out-of-clan Disciplines. And I hold it as a rule that very often certain Flaws consistently show up in certain lineages, so I can even cover different weaknesses as well (but in addition to the classic clan weakness, not as a substitute for it). But I often retain the flavor text for specific lineages of vampires, or certain groups within it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T16:19:24-07:00","id":1535044,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535030#post1535030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It is actually a canon &quot;fact&quot;, it is in the 1997 Dark Ages Companion, p. 171 <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I love to build my headcanons on quasy historical facts</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's why I loved it. Because the even is &quot;real&quot; and the idea the Witches got to their area of activity in this way is realistic. I'm toying with the idea that Set got Lilith's help in that war but kept it secret, and the Witches of Echidna were later eliminated for this reason, too.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535030#post1535030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I think that is the WoG and the WoE point of view or a heavy retcon<br /> Since other - older of course- material state around 13,000 BC the Settites appeared in Africa and both of Cairo by Night and Rage Across Egypt details and event called rhe Week of the Midnight Sun - it was a supernatural event that occurred in Egypt during the pre-Dynastic period, when Sakhmet, a legendary Simba warrior queen struck against those who dare oppose the light and embrace the dark god Set. Sakhmet's bond with the Celestine Ra was so great that her powers allowed her to force a red sun shine from the Umbra even in the night. The Week of the Midnight Sun was intended to end the dominion of Set. Sakhmet slaughtered the herds of the Setites, spread vile diseases, and fought several of his childer, among them the Scorpion King who she managed to destroy. The event drove the Setites into hiding, allowing the followers of Horus to conquer Upper Egypt and unite the Two Lands. (and secured a special place for the Bastet in the dark heart of Followers; the Bubasti helping Horus not helped them and they paid the price)<br /> The pre-Dynastic period was around 5000\u20133000 BC<br /> The oldest civilization in greece was the Minoan civilization, it was located on the island of Crete and existed from approximately 2700 BCE to 1450 BCE<br /> So the Followers and the veneration of Sutekh is deffinitly more older than their greece counterpart\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, it is either just their viewpoint or a heavy retcon. It could be either, frankly, but I read it more as a retcon. I also had a headcanon written down when I started playing the WoG, where Set was indeed Egyptian, but after the Embrace or after being poisoned by or diablerising Apep, he lost control of his Beast and went out of Egypt (which is also a thing in the older editions, a period in which he leaves Egypt and then returns), wandering through Asia Minor until he meets Echidna (sorcerer, possibly linked to Lilith) and Glycon (which I had counted as a son of Asclepius, the mythical doctor, for reasons I forgot), embraces them and they help regain control of the Beast. With Animalism, Echidna helps him put his Beast in the Typhonic Beast, which is then taken by the Gangrel and contained in the Ennoah's body, hence why Set wanted it back so badly and fought the Gangrels over it. Then he went back to Egypt and established the priest lineage, so the WoE and WoG would be older, but the priests got to be around Set for longer and saw him more in a perfect, &quot;divine&quot; state, while Echidna and Glycon saw him at his worst and helped him through that.<br /> <br /> But regardless of headcanons, with regards to the timeline between Egypt and Greece, my point is that the myths of Echidna and Typhon are considered to be quite older than Greek civilisation itself and to possibly be related to similar Mesopotamian and ancient Middle Eastern myths They're not even &quot;based&quot; in Greece, since Echidna is said to be based in Asia Minor and Typhon also has a place in modern day Turkey as one of the places linked with his growtth or imprisonment (another place given for its imprisonment in Mount Etna, but then we would need to look into the Lasombra Antediluvian as well... and it's totally doable but it starts getting really messy). So Minoan civilisation is not as significant as a temporal reference.<br /> Also, snake cults (and WoE and WoG are much closer to proper snake cults than the Egyptian priests, since Set isn't even linked to snakes mythologically, except for his fight with Apep and the fact that EVERY Egyptian god could be represented by a snake determiner in hyeroglyphs, if memory serves me well, and maybe they all had snake forms in the primordial waters before creation) are generally considered extremely old in the history of religion, if I'm not wrong. So this would be my basis for saying that, whatever history the Setites have in Egypt, it is not impossible for the other two lines (and their understanding of Setism as a religion) to be older. Although, granted, in that case focus on Sutekh would likely not be just a Hellenistic product.<br /> It could be though that, since Set was in torpor during the Hellenistic period and disappeared in 33 bCE or CE, obsession with Set as an Egyptian deity might have been indeed a Hellenistic product of Egyptian philosopher-priests trying to prop up the prestige of Egyptian traditions against the growing popularity and prestige of Greek culture in Egypt. Before then, when Set was still awake and active, the clan might have been less fixated on that Egyptian deity, and Set/Sutekh might have been simply another local mask of the Setite Antediluvian - one of many, that did not conflict with the religious belief and practices of Setites (of any bloodline) outside of Egypt, where the Setite Antediluvian was represented through other masks and served through other rites.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T16:38:24-07:00","id":1535045,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535036#post1535036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There has been so many retcons and changes to the Setites over the years that it's hard to reconcile everything. The Setites were established with some very specific lore that got in the way of viewing them as just another clan, so Revised made a lot of changes to them and V5 did even more. A lot of the uniqueness of the Setites were thus lost. So you'll need to decide what to do when trying to incorporate canon into your own ideas because canon can be contradictory. I tend to default to the earlier canon myself when these things come up.<br /> <br /> If you go by the original description of the clan, it's important to note that around 900 BC, Set was defeated in Egypt, driven into exile, and his followers slaughtered. At that point, Set reviewed what had gone wrong and decided to make changes. The Followers of Set, as a clan with a culture and organization that we recognize today, is a result of that. In other words, the Setites were different prior to 900 BC. They were probably more like other clans, and the temple/cell based structure or corrupting things behind the scenes was adopted. It isn't clear whether all other Setites were destroyed by this time, though I think it's implied to be the case. It is also unclear whether all the other Setites were destroyed by Set's enemies, or whether Set &quot;cleaned house&quot; by eliminating any remaining Setites that he didn't find up to snuff. I imagine there could have been some Setites who survived the attacks by their enemies and were not purged by Set.<br /> <br /> So if I was going to include any Setite bloodlines that existed during the Dark Ages and/or today, I would only have them be created after 900 BC. Another option would be to link one or both bloodlines to the previous generation of Setites before 900 BC. If so, that bloodline might be seen as heretical since Set did not want them to survive. They represent the previous mistakes that caused his earlier failure. That would explain why that bloodline(s) later went extinct &quot;again&quot; - the other Setites wiped them out since they are not part of Set's master plan. Obviously, using this option would create a very different dynamic for characters who are of this bloodline. It would also explain why this bloodline is now part of the Anarchs rather than aligned with the actual Clan.<br /> <br /> Another thing I would probably do is trace any new characters of such bloodlines to a single survivor recently awakened from torpor. The bloodlines did seem to go extinct, but since all it takes is one survivor to create new ones, it's easy to come back. A single character in long term torpor who woke up is an easy explanation of what happened between then and now. Put your creative energy into making that single survivor interesting and how that character is viewed and interacted with by its relatively few descendants.<br /> <br /> Personally, I have never liked the idea of sub-clans - those bloodlines that are members of the actual clan but had different in-clan Disciplines or different clan weaknesses. I just make them all mechanically the same. It's not like vampires cannot learn out-of-clan Disciplines. And I hold it as a rule that very often certain Flaws consistently show up in certain lineages, so I can even cover different weaknesses as well (but in addition to the classic clan weakness, not as a substitute for it). But I often retain the flavor text for specific lineages of vampires, or certain groups within it.</div> </div> </div> </div>The idea of the genesis of other bloodline dating back to 900 BCE is very good, I think. Another option - or another one that can go with it in the same metaplot - is using the sinking of Tanis: several ancients were lost under the waters, and of 13 clan leaders, only 7 remained. The identities of the remaining 6 are a mystery, as is their fate under the waters. Could they have been from the WoE or the WoG?<br /> <br /> As for bloodlines as sub-clans, I get what you mean. I think overall the clan/bloodline system could do with some tweaks, but Requiem and V5 seem to have already gone partly in that direction. Personally, I never minded sub-clans with slightly different disciplines. When the clan weakness changes, though, I think it suggests something deeper must have occurred. The three castes of Banu Haqim have very similar weaknesses - it comes down to make their focus easily known through Aura reading, plus the skin darkening. As for the 3 VDA20 Setite bloodlines, they have very different weaknesses, and I think this can suggest weirder origins than one might expect. In another headcanon I exposed in another thread, I have the WoG and WoE as actually descending from Lilith, with related but distinct origins, so the weakness of both is &quot;hunger&quot;, but WoG hunger for power and faith/conversions, while WoE hunger for blood. Regular Followers of Set descend instead from the Setite Antediluvian who diablerised or did something dirty to the founders of the other two bloodlines, so they got quite a different weakness but similar powers (Serpentis as in clan plus sorcery as widely taught). For some reason, however, at some point the three start functioning as a single clan.<br /> <br /> Different weaknesses and disciplines can also suggest the founder of a clan isn't really a thrd gen, but a second gen whose descendants were coordinated enough to appear socially as a single clan rather than 3, each wih their own Antediluvian.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-01T20:09:16-07:00","id":1535049,"title":"","content":"So in my games I tend to use the idea of Typhon-Set. That is, Set was embraced by the vampire Typhon. Eventually Set diablarized Typhon. Typhon's soul and Set struggled for supremacy for thousands of years, and sometimes Typhon would end up in charge, sometimes Set. Depending on who was in charge at a specific point in time, they would create a new bloodline, or wipe out the followers of the other one. Eventually Set manages to become ascendent and wipes out the Witches, which were loyal to the Typhonic aspect.<br /> <br /> Then for the Witches of Echidna specifically, I have them as something of a Bahari cult. In mythology Echidna, the Mother of Monsters, was Typhon's wife. And there's a surprising amount of overlap between the mythology of Echidna and Lilith, especially in terms of giving birth to powerful monsters. Set claims to be the first vampire, but if you were to consider Typhon the first vampire, then he would be analogous to Caine and his wife Echidna would be analogous with Lilith. Maybe the Witches were a proto-Bahari cult, or maybe they were influenced by the Bahari. Either way in my games the Witches are closely allied with the Bahari, to the point that outsiders often can't tell the difference. Ultimately a handful of Witches managed to survive extermination by hiding among the Bahari.<br /> <br /> I tend to run the Witches niche as seers, cursers and monster makers. I very much have them play into the classical &quot;wicked witch&quot; archetype, pulling from stories like MacBeth or Beowulf (Grendel's Mother). Mechanically one of their unique tricks is a line of Thaumaturgy and Rituals that allow them to birth monsters or turn beings into monsters. They are closely allied with Bane spirits, and can manifest them across the Gauntlet or twist creatures into Fomori. Their most powerful Ritual allows them to give birth to a being that's essentially a Revenant/Fomori.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-02T00:43:09-07:00","id":1535054,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535028#post1535028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hellenistic Egypt's syncretism might be the culprit for Egyptian myths entering the clan, and attempts may have been made to make Setism look more ancient as a religion by banking on the reputation of Egyptian myths and rituals as being older than Greece's own. This is my speculation.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>And a very good one!<br /> One of my personal favorite is to place Sutekh' point of origin in to Scandinavia, not Egyt and from the line of Canarl, theprogenitor of the Einherjar, not Caine<br /> Strangely, while Sutekh have the face of the Sha, the Set Animal or the &quot;Typhonic beast.&quot; he was almost in every description described as a very tall guy with white skin, flaming red long hair and green eyes<br /> Egyptians are... well not really look like that; not that tall, certanly not had white skin, green eyes or red hair, tho' the later two time to time appeared, like in the case of Ramses<br /> Sutekh is more like and Irish or Scandinavian guy<br /> And we have a tall, red haired green eyed white skinned warrior who command the thunder in the mithology in that area...<br /> An area that is so heavy with fae, shapechanger, gods, elves and everything you can't toss a brick without hiting one on the head + an all devouring sperpent just for a bonus","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-02T02:39:12-07:00","id":1535057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535054#post1535054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> And a very good one!<br /> One of my personal favorite is to place Sutekh' point of origin in to Scandinavia, not Egyt and from the line of Canarl, theprogenitor of the Einherjar, not Caine<br /> Strangely, while Sutekh have the face of the Sha, the Set Animal or the &quot;Typhonic beast.&quot; he was almost in every description described as a very tall guy with white skin, flaming red long hair and green eyes<br /> Egyptians are... well not really look like that; not that tall, certanly not had white skin, green eyes or red hair, tho' the later two time to time appeared, like in the case of Ramses<br /> Sutekh is more like and Irish or Scandinavian guy<br /> And we have a tall, red haired green eyed white skinned warrior who command the thunder in the mithology in that area...<br /> An area that is so heavy with fae, shapechanger, gods, elves and everything you can't toss a brick without hiting one on the head + an all devouring sperpent just for a bonus</div> </div> </div> </div>Love it. I had never thought of a possible northern European origin, but it also has a lot of potential. And I think it must be remembered that Sutekh is the god of strangers... so much so that Egyptians offended by the Jewish Bible's Exodus associated Yahweh with Set - and this was when Set was already seen quite negatively. \u0130 think a couple of Hyksos pharaohs also had Sutekh in their royal name - quite unusual. Or maybe it was Apep? Maybe Apep. Either way, Sutekh as a stranger to Egypt would be very fitting.<br /> <br /> Thor dies poisoned battling Jormungandr during the Ragnarok. Setites say Set was defending Ra and fighting Apep when he was poisoned by the latter and transformed into a creature of the Duat (VDA20 core manual, Apocripha of the Clan: Followers of Set). Basically, Thor dies poisoned at the end of an age and Sutekh is born.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-02T13:34:11-07:00","id":1535085,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535054#post1535054\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Egyptians are... well not really look like that; not that tall, certanly not had white skin, green eyes or red hair, tho' the later two time to time appeared, like in the case of Ramses</div> </div> </div> </div>I have to disagree here. The native peoples of North Africa and the Middle East are, in fact, considered &quot;white&quot; in so far they are classified among the standard racial types. There are plenty of Berbers, Arabs, Copts, Kurds, Lebanese, etc. that could be mistaken for Europeans. This is like saying because Sicilians and Greeks don't look like Scandinavians, they aren't white either. Compared to sub-Saharan Africans, the Egyptians would have definitely considered themselves to have lighter skins. And though rare, native Berbers, Copts, Arabs, etc. can have red hair and blue or green eyes as you mentioned.<br /> <br /> Simply because in your imagination you first visualize light skinned, red hair, and green eyed people with northern Europe (and to be honest, I do as well) does not mean the Egyptians weren't thinking of themselves when imagining how their gods looked. They certainly weren't thinking of far away, barbarous Scandinavians whom they may not have even known about.<br /> <br /> I'll admit, I haven't actually read before that Set himself had red hair or green eyes. I'm not familiar with any actual Egyptian texts, and the version of their mythology I've read doesn't mention it. Since his head is that of an animal (either the unknown set-animal or donkey among others), I've never given it much thought other than he had a donkey for a head. I know that red haired people were considered sacred to Set, but that is probably because Set was the lord of the &quot;red land&quot; meaning the desert, and the Egyptians associated the color red with chaos and disorder in general which Set ruled over. So it'd be natural for Egyptians to associate the few red headed people they knew as being favored by Set - they are marked with his color.<br /> <br /> Having said that, there's no reason as a survivor of antedeluvian world that no longer exists that Set or the other antedeluvians can't look like anything you want. I assume most of us default to a generic Middle Eastern appearance for the antedeluvians because of its Biblical references, but they could appear with a variety in their appearance, even perhaps with combinations that would seem very exotic or unnatural to our eyes because current people don't have their combination of colors and looks. Of course, if you insist that the antedeluvians do not come from some pre-Deluge world but some historical period (and some STs do) then they could come from anywhere.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-02T14:37:55-07:00","id":1535089,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535085#post1535085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I have to disagree here. The native peoples of North Africa and the Middle East are, in fact, considered &quot;white&quot; in so far they are classified among the standard racial types. There are plenty of Berbers, Arabs, Copts, Kurds, Lebanese, etc. that could be mistaken for Europeans. This is like saying because Sicilians and Greeks don't look like Scandinavians, they aren't white either. Compared to sub-Saharan Africans, the Egyptians would have definitely considered themselves to have lighter skins. And though rare, native Berbers, Copts, Arabs, etc. can have red hair and blue or green eyes as you mentioned.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your point is not wrong, even though some of the people you mentioned are not native to Egypt. But then, by the time written records become available, humans had already been doing a lot of stuff and travelling extensively, so I put very little stock in the idea of &quot;native people&quot; if that's being used to deny the frequent and very ancient mixing of human populations. Also, there are some mummies and depictions of pharaohs and the like with red hair, so we may be wrong indeed in assuming the first Egyptians did not see any except if they came from a country as far as Scandinavia. I think <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar23890_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"23890\" data-vbnamecard=\"23890\">Shadeprowler</a> 's idea however can be ok for narrative and entertainment.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535085#post1535085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I'll admit, I haven't actually read before that Set himself had red hair or green eyes. I'm not familiar with any actual Egyptian texts, and the version of their mythology I've read doesn't mention it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have not found an exact source, but I'm more inclined to think the red hair thing was in Egyptian texts or depictions, and not just because of being lord of the red land. Some Pharaoh are known to have had red hair, maybe including Seti, whose name comes from Set.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535085#post1535085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Since his head is that of an animal (either the unknown set-animal or donkey among others), I've never given it much thought other than he had a donkey for a head.</div> </div> </div> </div>Egyptian deities did not actually have animal heads. They are portrayed in multiple forms, including purely human (Set included), and some text of the scribes consacrated to Thoth, who put extra emphasis on the religious mysteries of language and scripture, gives the idea that the various animals were just metaphors for the deties' qualities, since the language itself relied on images (through hyeroglyphs) and sometimes there were sort of puns based on how a hyeroglyph was pronounced (which we mainly guess) and other words that sounded similar. The Greeks and the Romans, looking at Egyptian deities, imagined they were actually part animals, and thought that to be quite silly, but from what I have learned so far on ancient Egypt's religion, that idea would be a naive reading of Egyptian language and theology. It's also very possible, though, that, while the scribes initiated into the service of Thoth knew the puns and metaphors involved in the signs used to represent the gods, uneducated people didn't and took things rather literally.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535085#post1535085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Having said that, there's no reason as a survivor of antedeluvian world that no longer exists that Set or the other antedeluvians can't look like anything you want. I assume most of us default to a generic Middle Eastern appearance for the antedeluvians because of its Biblical references, but they could appear with a variety in their appearance, even perhaps with combinations that would seem very exotic or unnatural to our eyes because current people don't have their combination of colors and looks. Of course, if you insist that the antedeluvians do not come from some pre-Deluge world but some historical period (and some STs do) then they could come from anywhere.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Set is likely to have Obfuscate 10, so he should be able to influence how much and what people remember of him anyway, appearance included.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-02T23:04:16-07:00","id":1535097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535085#post1535085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I have to disagree here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fair points but I don't think they are <i>copletly</i> accurate<br /> <br /> The renowned egyptologist Maspero has written: ''Set-Typhon was red-haired and white-skinned, of violent, gloomy, and jealous temper.'' (History of Egypt, 1903, p. 127).<br /> <br /> The Ebers Papyrus (c. 1550 BC) describes the followers of Set (Seth) as red haired and fair skinned (cf. Beitr\u00e4ge zur \u00c4ltesten Geschichte \u00c4gyptens, 1903, p. 127; Wainright, 1938, p. 31).<br /> <br /> A papyrus dating to the 13th century BC (Dynasty XIX) describes the ''followers of Seth'' as redheads (Hieratic Papyri in the British Museum, Third Series, i, Text, pp. 10, 20, 21).<br /> <br /> A fragment from the Dream Book of Papyrus Chester Beatty III describes Seth as red haired (quoted in The Ancient Egyptians, Adolf Erman, 1978, p. xv).<br /> <br /> In the ''Festival Songs of Isis and Nephyths'' the followers of Seth are called the ''red ones'' after their red hair (Bremner-Rhind P. I, 5, 1 cf. Faulkner, HEA 22, 125, 135).<br /> <br /> The Pyramid Texts (c. 2300 BC) describe Seth as ''white faced'' (cf. Beitr\u00e4ge zur \u00c4ltesten Geschichte \u00c4gyptens, Pyr. 1659 b) while describing his eyes as light or bright.<br /> <br /> Diodorus Siculus (1st century BC) describes Typhon (Set) as red-haired (i. 88. 5).<br /> <br /> Plutarch (1st century AD) notes that Typhon (Set) had red hair (De Iside et Osiride, 362. xxx).<br /> <br /> Petrie in his Religion and Conscience in Ancient Egypt (1898) notes ''That Set belongs to the Libyans or Westerns is probable, because he is considered to have red hair and a white skin.'' (p. 32).<br /> <br /> Margaret Bunsen in her Encylopedia of Ancient Egypt (2002) notes that the followers of Seth ''were called 'the red ones' because they supposedly had red faces and red hair'' (p. 139).<br /> <br /> <br /> Of course when we are talking about gods, it is mater of interpretation, especially in a symbolic relegion (witch half of the time contradict itself) like the Egyptian but Seth was always the outsider, the antithesis of the quintessential Egyptian man as portrayed in Horus<br /> And you are right, mMost scholars believe that Egyptians in antiquity looked pretty much as they look today, with a gradation of darker shades toward the Sudan&quot;. Christopher Ehret wrote in 1996: &quot;Ancient Egyptian civilization was, in ways and to an extent usually not recognized, fundamentally African.<br /> But there is a difference between &quot;white&quot; and light skinned<br /> <br /> Anyhow, I just mentioned that as a basis of Set's northern European origin, for the fun possibility <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-03T06:02:33-07:00","id":1535101,"title":"","content":"Just addding to the discussion the fact that red hair seems (according to Wikipedia) to have originated in Central Asia, although it is more common among Northern Europeans.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-03T21:17:04-07:00","id":1535130,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535049#post1535049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So in my games I tend to use the idea of Typhon-Set. That is, Set was embraced by the vampire Typhon. Eventually Set diablarized Typhon. Typhon's soul and Set struggled for supremacy for thousands of years, and sometimes Typhon would end up in charge, sometimes Set. Depending on who was in charge at a specific point in time, they would create a new bloodline, or wipe out the followers of the other one. Eventually Set manages to become ascendent and wipes out the Witches, which were loyal to the Typhonic aspect.<br /> <br /> Then for the Witches of Echidna specifically, I have them as something of a Bahari cult. In mythology Echidna, the Mother of Monsters, was Typhon's wife. And there's a surprising amount of overlap between the mythology of Echidna and Lilith, especially in terms of giving birth to powerful monsters. Set claims to be the first vampire, but if you were to consider Typhon the first vampire, then he would be analogous to Caine and his wife Echidna would be analogous with Lilith. Maybe the Witches were a proto-Bahari cult, or maybe they were influenced by the Bahari. Either way in my games the Witches are closely allied with the Bahari, to the point that outsiders often can't tell the difference. Ultimately a handful of Witches managed to survive extermination by hiding among the Bahari.<br /> <br /> I tend to run the Witches niche as seers, cursers and monster makers. I very much have them play into the classical &quot;wicked witch&quot; archetype, pulling from stories like MacBeth or Beowulf (Grendel's Mother). Mechanically one of their unique tricks is a line of Thaumaturgy and Rituals that allow them to birth monsters or turn beings into monsters. They are closely allied with Bane spirits, and can manifest them across the Gauntlet or twist creatures into Fomori. Their most powerful Ritual allows them to give birth to a being that's essentially a Revenant/Fomori.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just quoting myself since my post spent several days being digested by the spam filter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T05:45:00-07:00","id":1535138,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535101#post1535101\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just addding to the discussion the fact that red hair seems (according to Wikipedia) to have originated in Central Asia, although it is more common among Northern Europeans.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535130#post1535130\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just quoting myself since my post spent several days being digested by the spam filter.</div> </div> </div> </div>This gave me an interesting idea<br /> The Land of Nod was located east of Eden where Caine was exiled by God after the murder of his brother Abel.<br /> Eden<br /> Okay, just find the Garden of Eden...<br /> <br /> \u201cA river watering the garden flowed from Eden; from there it was separated into four headwaters. The name of the first is the Pishon; it winds through the entire land of Havilah, where there is gold. (The gold of that land is good; aromatic resin and onyx are also there.) The name of the second river is the Gihon; it winds through the entire land of Cush. The name of the third river is the Tigris; it runs along the east side of Ashur. And the fourth river is the Euphrates.\u201d<br /> <br /> Two of these rivers, the Tigris and Euphrates, still flow today. The Pishon and the Gihon dried up soon after the close of the last ice age. All four rivers flow or flowed into the Persian Gulf. They come close together in the southeastern part of today\u2019s Persian Gulf. Presently, this location is about 200 feet below sea level. During most of the last ice age, it was above sea level.<br /> There are many speculations about where the land of Nod may be, but it is safe to say, the Land of Nod was somewhere the area of today's Afghanistan and Pakistan - the area's geography would suit the WoD description, not to mention the First City, Enoch there in the Toreador interpretation was called Ubar, meaning &quot;Queen of Frankincense.&quot;<br /> Frankincense is an aromatic resin from the Boswellia sacra tree, and from <b>India, and the Middle East</b>, frankincense oil has been important both socially and economically as an ingredient in incense and perfumes for thousands of years<br /> Not to mention would make all those Antediluvan travels to the East much more realistic, and would explain why there is a strong Cainite presence in India to the point the Danava believe themselves to be the guardians of their Antediluvian (pitri, or &quot;father&quot;), whom they call Veddhartha. These Danava claim that the Ventrue are in fact an errant antitribu bloodline of the Danava. The Salubri say that Saulot achieved a transcendent spiritual state in the Indian city of Golconda, which falls within territory controlled by the Danava - some even believe that the very notion of Golconda originated from the Danava<br /> Now this little sidetrack, let me explain my theory, built on these!<br /> Saulot and Set said to be brothers (and with the triplets Malkav, Malakai, and Arikel and their sister Nathema,) <br /> Saulot demonstreated a very strong dual personality, Zao-Lat and Saulot, the White Worm and the the Prophet, Hun and his P'o separated - the Salubri even have a quesy-canon option to transform their beast to an awakened P'o and start their Dharmic journy<br /> Why? Because the Land of Nod basically was the part of the Middle Kingdom or at least was on the border to be effected by the &quot;laws of the Middle Kingdom and the Yama Kings&quot;<br /> Maybe his brother Set/Sutekh is similar - and to be fair, Malkav, Malakai, and Arikel and their sister Nathema not that &quot;sane&quot; either, one even became a Demon (ascended to a Yama Queen perhaps?)<br /> Set and Typhon , a &quot;good&quot; (or at leats not THAT bad) and a bad side - witch is witch is up for interpretation - but would align nicely AnubisXy theory too<br /> Set story how he returned from the udnerword is very similar of the Kuei-jin creation how someone return from Yomi <br /> Maybe Set and Saulot walked the same path with the same results - and this is mirrored in their Clan.The Witches and Warriors were replaced just like the Salubri and consantly evolved depending on what personality the rational Hun or the demonci P'o was dominant, just in Set case he stayed in one body<br /> \u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T15:58:28-07:00","id":1535151,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AnubisXy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535049#post1535049\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So in my games I tend to use the idea of Typhon-Set. That is, Set was embraced by the vampire Typhon. Eventually Set diablarized Typhon. Typhon's soul and Set struggled for supremacy for thousands of years, and sometimes Typhon would end up in charge, sometimes Set. Depending on who was in charge at a specific point in time, they would create a new bloodline, or wipe out the followers of the other one. Eventually Set manages to become ascendent and wipes out the Witches, which were loyal to the Typhonic aspect.<br /> <br /> Then for the Witches of Echidna specifically, I have them as something of a Bahari cult. In mythology Echidna, the Mother of Monsters, was Typhon's wife. And there's a surprising amount of overlap between the mythology of Echidna and Lilith, especially in terms of giving birth to powerful monsters. Set claims to be the first vampire, but if you were to consider Typhon the first vampire, then he would be analogous to Caine and his wife Echidna would be analogous with Lilith. Maybe the Witches were a proto-Bahari cult, or maybe they were influenced by the Bahari. Either way in my games the Witches are closely allied with the Bahari, to the point that outsiders often can't tell the difference. Ultimately a handful of Witches managed to survive extermination by hiding among the Bahari.<br /> <br /> I tend to run the Witches niche as seers, cursers and monster makers. I very much have them play into the classical &quot;wicked witch&quot; archetype, pulling from stories like MacBeth or Beowulf (Grendel's Mother). Mechanically one of their unique tricks is a line of Thaumaturgy and Rituals that allow them to birth monsters or turn beings into monsters. They are closely allied with Bane spirits, and can manifest them across the Gauntlet or twist creatures into Fomori. Their most powerful Ritual allows them to give birth to a being that's essentially a Revenant/Fomori.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we spoke about this also in another thread. I like that theory, too. In that thread I think I also posted an idea linking Typhon and Echidna with the children of Lilith in D'hanu, and Set as a childe of Caine who messed up Lilith's Garden with the other Antediluvians. The three Setite bloodlines would come from Typhon (Warriors of Glycon), Echidna (Witches) and Set (Priests) respectively, with a messy history of infights and clan redefinition depending on how the soul-battle between Set and Typhon was going, like in your idea.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude18","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T16:12:12-07:00","id":1535152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535138#post1535138\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This gave me an interesting idea<br /> The Land of Nod was located east of Eden where Caine was exiled by God after the murder of his brother Abel.<br /> Eden<br /> Okay, just find the Garden of Eden...<br /> <br /> \u201cA river watering the garden flowed from Eden; from there it was separated into four headwaters. The name of the first is the Pishon; it winds through the entire land of Havilah, where there is gold. (The gold of that land is good; aromatic resin and onyx are also there.) The name of the second river is the Gihon; it winds through the entire land of Cush. The name of the third river is the Tigris; it runs along the east side of Ashur. And the fourth river is the Euphrates.\u201d<br /> <br /> Two of these rivers, the Tigris and Euphrates, still flow today. The Pishon and the Gihon dried up soon after the close of the last ice age. All four rivers flow or flowed into the Persian Gulf. They come close together in the southeastern part of today\u2019s Persian Gulf. Presently, this location is about 200 feet below sea level. During most of the last ice age, it was above sea level.<br /> There are many speculations about where the land of Nod may be, but it is safe to say, the Land of Nod was somewhere the area of today's Afghanistan and Pakistan - the area's geography would suit the WoD description, not to mention the First City, Enoch there in the Toreador interpretation was called Ubar, meaning &quot;Queen of Frankincense.&quot;<br /> Frankincense is an aromatic resin from the Boswellia sacra tree, and from <b>India, and the Middle East</b>, frankincense oil has been important both socially and economically as an ingredient in incense and perfumes for thousands of years<br /> Not to mention would make all those Antediluvan travels to the East much more realistic, and would explain why there is a strong Cainite presence in India to the point the Danava believe themselves to be the guardians of their Antediluvian (pitri, or &quot;father&quot;), whom they call Veddhartha. These Danava claim that the Ventrue are in fact an errant antitribu bloodline of the Danava. The Salubri say that Saulot achieved a transcendent spiritual state in the Indian city of Golconda, which falls within territory controlled by the Danava - some even believe that the very notion of Golconda originated from the Danava<br /> Now this little sidetrack, let me explain my theory, built on these!<br /> Saulot and Set said to be brothers (and with the triplets Malkav, Malakai, and Arikel and their sister Nathema,)<br /> Saulot demonstreated a very strong dual personality, Zao-Lat and Saulot, the White Worm and the the Prophet, Hun and his P'o separated - the Salubri even have a quesy-canon option to transform their beast to an awakened P'o and start their Dharmic journy<br /> Why? Because the Land of Nod basically was the part of the Middle Kingdom or at least was on the border to be effected by the &quot;laws of the Middle Kingdom and the Yama Kings&quot;<br /> Maybe his brother Set/Sutekh is similar - and to be fair, Malkav, Malakai, and Arikel and their sister Nathema not that &quot;sane&quot; either, one even became a Demon (ascended to a Yama Queen perhaps?)<br /> Set and Typhon , a &quot;good&quot; (or at leats not THAT bad) and a bad side - witch is witch is up for interpretation - but would align nicely AnubisXy theory too<br /> Set story how he returned from the udnerword is very similar of the Kuei-jin creation how someone return from Yomi<br /> Maybe Set and Saulot walked the same path with the same results - and this is mirrored in their Clan.The Witches and Warriors were replaced just like the Salubri and consantly evolved depending on what personality the rational Hun or the demonci P'o was dominant, just in Set case he stayed in one body<br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this. I would go for Set as good (because he guards the barque of Ra) and Typhon-Apep as bad. In other mythologies: Zeus/Typhon (both lightning and storm deities fighting, one benefic and orderly, the other malefic and chaotic), Thor/Jormungandr (or Loki). There were other similar couples in other mythologies, but I can't recall them now. <br /> <br /> Where is that option for the Salubri to start their Dharmic journey? It sounds very interesting. Gives me an idea to put Kuei-jin, Cainites and Laibon (with their system of Aye and Orun) on a fluid spectrum where they can switch from one spiritual system to another depending on certain factors, as if they were fundamentally the same creature.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T21:47:03-07:00","id":1535159,"title":"","content":"I haven't devled into the topic properly but from previous stuff posted by TS I'm looking forward for a thread with alot of good stuff about he Setites. Hell, as far as my opinion go I wouldn't mind to read official material written by TS on the Setites as a freelancer writer on VtM/DA.<br /> <br /> I hope you keep going strong for a long time. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/970-gurkhal","name":"Gurkhal","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"863"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-04T23:31:24-07:00","id":1535161,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535152#post1535152\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Where is that option for the Salubri to start their Dharmic journey? It sounds very interesting.</div> </div> </div> </div>On Blood &amp; Silk p. 158 there is a &quot;gossip&quot;, not a 100% fact, just &quot;rumors&quot; about the Salubri, the spawns of Zao-Lat and about their potential to awaken their P'o and start their Dharmic journey<br /> <br /> Since it is a sidebar thing and a &quot;gossip&quot;, I called it quasi-canon<br /> But since the Salubri Valeren/Obeah is the carbon copy of the Kuei-jin Soul Discipline <b>Chi'iu Muh </b>and Zao-Lat demonstarated a very strong Howl of the Devil-Tiger behavior with the creation of the Baali (or if we want to be more exact, I think the Way of Ten Thousand Screams is more likely Zao-Lat tried - it was the first Dharma before Xue's formulation of the Fivefold Way during the time of the Demon Warrior Empire, to advocate embracing the P'o in an attempt to achieve a perfect union with the Yama Kings. The reasoning behind this was that the Creator made them for a purpose - to keep the Great Cycle in motion. Those of the Ten Thousand Screams Dharma chose one of the Yama Kings to emulate and spent their unlives fulfilling their terrible purpose. This &quot;proto-dharma&quot; was hunted to extinction but its philosophy of harnessing the P'o were adapted by Xue in the Howl of the Devil-Tiger Dharma)<br /> So if any of my players as a Salubri for one reason or another would want to awaken their P'o? I let them do it. <br /> <br /> \u200bThe DA Flaw, Face of the Beast (2pt. Flaw) - The Beast Within emerges in times of hunger or stress, twisting your visage into a monstrous caricature of human form. - especially handy, if you plan something like this, mimicing a bit of the P'o's Demon Shintai Discipline that allows a Kuei-jin to manifest his Inner Demon, transforming his flesh to resemble the servants of Yomi <br /> Similar with the Beast in the Mirror (1 or 2pt. Flaw) Flaw - Whenever you stare into a reflective surface, you see your Beast leering back at you.<br /> <br /> Pair this with Demon-hounded (1 to 4pt. Flaw) - A demon from below has taken a special interest in you or Soul Eater (4pt. Flaw) - Your Beast hungers for more than blood. Whenever you feed, you steal one Willpower point from your victim for every three blood points taken and/or with the Merit Gifts of the Beast (Variable Cost Merit) - Your Beast has a terrible awareness and power all its own that emerges when its wrath or fear overtakes you. <br /> <br /> And you basically have a Kuei-jin ready to go","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-05T00:11:46-07:00","id":1535163,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535161#post1535161\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> On Blood &amp; Silk p. 158 there is a &quot;gossip&quot;, not a 100% fact, just &quot;rumors&quot; about the Salubri, the spawns of Zao-Lat and about their potential to awaken their P'o and start their Dharmic journey<br /> <br /> Since it is a sidebar thing and a &quot;gossip&quot;, I called it quasi-canon<br /> But since the Salubri Valeren/Obeah is the carbon copy of the Kuei-jin Soul Discipline <b>Chi'iu Muh </b>and Zao-Lat demonstarated a very strong Howl of the Devil-Tiger behavior with the creation of the Baali (or if we want to be more exact, I think the Way of Ten Thousand Screams is more likely Zao-Lat tried - it was the first Dharma before Xue's formulation of the Fivefold Way during the time of the Demon Warrior Empire, to advocate embracing the P'o in an attempt to achieve a perfect union with the Yama Kings. The reasoning behind this was that the Creator made them for a purpose - to keep the Great Cycle in motion. Those of the Ten Thousand Screams Dharma chose one of the Yama Kings to emulate and spent their unlives fulfilling their terrible purpose. This &quot;proto-dharma&quot; was hunted to extinction but its philosophy of harnessing the P'o were adapted by Xue in the Howl of the Devil-Tiger Dharma)<br /> So if any of my players as a Salubri for one reason or another would want to awaken their P'o? I let them do it.<br /> <br /> The DA Flaw, Face of the Beast (2pt. Flaw) - The Beast Within emerges in times of hunger or stress, twisting your visage into a monstrous caricature of human form. - especially handy, if you plan something like this, mimicing a bit of the P'o's Demon Shintai Discipline that allows a Kuei-jin to manifest his Inner Demon, transforming his flesh to resemble the servants of Yomi<br /> Similar with the Beast in the Mirror (1 or 2pt. Flaw) Flaw - Whenever you stare into a reflective surface, you see your Beast leering back at you.<br /> <br /> Pair this with Demon-hounded (1 to 4pt. Flaw) - A demon from below has taken a special interest in you or Soul Eater (4pt. Flaw) - Your Beast hungers for more than blood. Whenever you feed, you steal one Willpower point from your victim for every three blood points taken and/or with the Merit Gifts of the Beast (Variable Cost Merit) - Your Beast has a terrible awareness and power all its own that emerges when its wrath or fear overtakes you.<br /> <br /> And you basically have a Kuei-jin ready to go</div> </div> </div> </div>Very cool. Face of the Beast also reminds of part of the Witches of Echidna's clan weakness: whenever they check for frenzy, they roll to resist it twice (if they have Self-control). \u0130f they fail either roll, their face takes the visage of a tartarine demon (this was discussed in another thread=demon of Tartarus) for a turn. \u0130f they have \u0130nstinct, this transformation is automatic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-05T00:14:01-07:00","id":1535164,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gurkhal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535159#post1535159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven't devled into the topic properly but from previous stuff posted by TS I'm looking forward for a thread with alot of good stuff about he Setites. Hell, as far as my opinion go I wouldn't mind to read official material written by TS on the Setites as a freelancer writer on VtM/DA.<br /> <br /> I hope you keep going strong for a long time. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>Oh thank you!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-08T23:43:23-07:00","id":1535334,"title":"","content":"I am LOVING the idea of the Setites having started out as a Hellenic Typhon cult. It provides an excuse for the differences between the Set of real-world history and the snake cult of the game line, while restoring a bit of mystery to the ancient world. (Seriously, for a founder of a clan known for its use of Obfuscate, Set sure gets a lot of stories told about him. Maybe it's more appropriate if his worshippers have just been inserting myth and legend to fill the gaps in what they know about where their blood comes from.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11599-reasor","name":"Reasor","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-09T03:21:19-07:00","id":1535337,"title":"","content":"It is interesting tho', the Lasombra Antediluvian was worshipped as a God by the Sea Peoples, among them the Hyksos<br /> Now a little twist!<br /> The Hyksos King <b>Apepi</b> (or greek name, Apophis... ) or Nebkhepeshre, ruler of Lower Egypt during the Fifteenth Dynasty and the end of the Second Intermediate Period. is recorded as worshiping Set exclusively, as described in the following passage:<br /> &quot;King Apophis chose for his Lord the god Seth. He did not worship any other deity in the whole land except Seth&quot; - &quot;The Quarrel of Apophis and Seqenenre&quot;, Papyrus Sallier I, 1.2\u20133 (British Museum No. 10185)<br /> <br /> AND Serpentis havea... strange power, called: <b>Shadow of Apep. </b>Using it the Follower of Set become a nearly indestructible giant snake of <i>pure darkness</i><br /> <br /> One must wonder if the Laza Omri Baras, the Lasombra Antediluvian and Sutekh is... one and the same?<br /> Woudl explain this strange coincidence<br /> Not to mention the whole Tlacique Bloodline. They claim, their founder is/was Tezcatlipoca, who was a central deity in Aztec religion. He was associated with a variety of concepts, including the night sky, hurricanes, obsidian, and conflict. <br /> This is basically Sutekh's portfolio<br /> H\u00e9ctor de Velasco, one of the first Sabbat in the New World, claimed Tezcatlipoca to be a &quot;reflection of what the mirror would not show others&quot; and the &quot;cast-off countenance of a Blooded being so powerful even its reflection refused to fade&quot; and was able to create solid objects from darkness that withstood even sunlight and using shadow-like proboscis to feed itself from subjects. While in his temple, he was even able to be active during the day <br /> <br /> This is a bit similar again, to the Serpentis power of <b>Pact with Ra</b>: You &quot;remind&quot; Ra, Set was his champion and defender and walk in the sun without harm for one day<br /> <br /> Od course, the Tlacique could be a Drowned Legacy... but Tezcatlipoca as Sutekh or at least a Child of Sutekh is an interesting possibility, especially if the Lasombra Antediluvian and Sutekh are basically the same person. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-09T09:52:23-07:00","id":1535357,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535337#post1535337\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It is interesting tho', the Lasombra Antediluvian was worshipped as a God by the Sea Peoples, among them the Hyksos<br /> Now a little twist!<br /> The Hyksos King <b>Apepi</b> (or greek name, Apophis... ) or Nebkhepeshre, ruler of Lower Egypt during the Fifteenth Dynasty and the end of the Second Intermediate Period. is recorded as worshiping Set exclusively, as described in the following passage:<br /> &quot;King Apophis chose for his Lord the god Seth. He did not worship any other deity in the whole land except Seth&quot; - &quot;The Quarrel of Apophis and Seqenenre&quot;, Papyrus Sallier I, 1.2\u20133 (British Museum No. 10185)<br /> <br /> AND Serpentis havea... strange power, called: <b>Shadow of Apep. </b>Using it the Follower of Set become a nearly indestructible giant snake of <i>pure darkness</i><br /> <br /> One must wonder if the Laza Omri Baras, the Lasombra Antediluvian and Sutekh is... one and the same?<br /> Woudl explain this strange coincidence<br /> Not to mention the whole Tlacique Bloodline. They claim, their founder is/was Tezcatlipoca, who was a central deity in Aztec religion. He was associated with a variety of concepts, including the night sky, hurricanes, obsidian, and conflict.<br /> This is basically Sutekh's portfolio<br /> H\u00e9ctor de Velasco, one of the first Sabbat in the New World, claimed Tezcatlipoca to be a &quot;reflection of what the mirror would not show others&quot; and the &quot;cast-off countenance of a Blooded being so powerful even its reflection refused to fade&quot; and was able to create solid objects from darkness that withstood even sunlight and using shadow-like proboscis to feed itself from subjects. While in his temple, he was even able to be active during the day<br /> <br /> This is a bit similar again, to the Serpentis power of <b>Pact with Ra</b>: You &quot;remind&quot; Ra, Set was his champion and defender and walk in the sun without harm for one day<br /> <br /> Od course, the Tlacique could be a Drowned Legacy... but Tezcatlipoca as Sutekh or at least a Child of Sutekh is an interesting possibility, especially if the Lasombra Antediluvian and Sutekh are basically the same person.</div> </div> </div> </div>Add to all that the fact that the Typhon of myth is said to trapped under Mount Etna (so, not far from [Lasombra]'s fortress), and that Apophis' job is to try to devour the Sun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-28T13:25:31-07:00","id":1536464,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535036#post1535036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There has been so many retcons and changes to the Setites over the years that it's hard to reconcile everything. The Setites were established with some very specific lore that got in the way of viewing them as just another clan, so Revised made a lot of changes to them and V5 did even more. A lot of the uniqueness of the Setites were thus lost. So you'll need to decide what to do when trying to incorporate canon into your own ideas because canon can be contradictory. I tend to default to the earlier canon myself when these things come up.<br /> <br /> If you go by the original description of the clan, it's important to note that around 900 BC, Set was defeated in Egypt, driven into exile, and his followers slaughtered. At that point, Set reviewed what had gone wrong and decided to make changes. The Followers of Set, as a clan with a culture and organization that we recognize today, is a result of that. In other words, the Setites were different prior to 900 BC. They were probably more like other clans, and the temple/cell based structure or corrupting things behind the scenes was adopted. It isn't clear whether all other Setites were destroyed by this time, though I think it's implied to be the case. It is also unclear whether all the other Setites were destroyed by Set's enemies, or whether Set &quot;cleaned house&quot; by eliminating any remaining Setites that he didn't find up to snuff. I imagine there could have been some Setites who survived the attacks by their enemies and were not purged by Set.<br /> <br /> So if I was going to include any Setite bloodlines that existed during the Dark Ages and/or today, I would only have them be created after 900 BC. Another option would be to link one or both bloodlines to the previous generation of Setites before 900 BC. If so, that bloodline might be seen as heretical since Set did not want them to survive. They represent the previous mistakes that caused his earlier failure. That would explain why that bloodline(s) later went extinct &quot;again&quot; - the other Setites wiped them out since they are not part of Set's master plan. Obviously, using this option would create a very different dynamic for characters who are of this bloodline. It would also explain why this bloodline is now part of the Anarchs rather than aligned with the actual Clan.<br /> <br /> Another thing I would probably do is trace any new characters of such bloodlines to a single survivor recently awakened from torpor. The bloodlines did seem to go extinct, but since all it takes is one survivor to create new ones, it's easy to come back. A single character in long term torpor who woke up is an easy explanation of what happened between then and now. Put your creative energy into making that single survivor interesting and how that character is viewed and interacted with by its relatively few descendants.<br /> <br /> Personally, I have never liked the idea of sub-clans - those bloodlines that are members of the actual clan but had different in-clan Disciplines or different clan weaknesses. I just make them all mechanically the same. It's not like vampires cannot learn out-of-clan Disciplines. And I hold it as a rule that very often certain Flaws consistently show up in certain lineages, so I can even cover different weaknesses as well (but in addition to the classic clan weakness, not as a substitute for it). But I often retain the flavor text for specific lineages of vampires, or certain groups within it.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is interesting, as I've recently started to write the Reunion Chronicle, where I want to tie up loose ends of now Clan Hecata. One of the motiffs that keeps coming back from various sources are links between Clan of Death and Setites, particularly Nagaraja. Around 900 BC a group of Death Mages (Euthanatoi) in India performed a ritual using Setites blood to become vampites. As the oldest known Nagaraja is a 5th Gen Mokhadaji Ranpur, it would suggest that they used a mathuselah's blood.<br /> <br /> Coincidentally, around 900 BC Kemintiri was driven to torpor and brought by Khetamon to India (Children of Osiris have discipline Bardo, another link to Indian subcontinent), so they could &quot;get rid of her beast&quot;. They didn't succeed but they brought her back from the bring of becoming a wight (source: Kindred Most Wanted), and in a weird &quot;blood transfusion&quot; ritual Nagaraja were born.<br /> <br /> Kemintiri hates Set, as he tortured her and took away the possibility of becoming a mummy by embracing her. She also wants to destroy the followers of Set, which fits with the origin of the Nagaraja\u2019s hatred against the Setites. So yeah, I agree that someting major must have happened to the Setites during that time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27765-euderos","name":"Euderos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27765_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"14"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-28T17:45:25-07:00","id":1536475,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Reasor</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1535334#post1535334\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am LOVING the idea of the Setites having started out as a Hellenic Typhon cult. It provides an excuse for the differences between the Set of real-world history and the snake cult of the game line, while restoring a bit of mystery to the ancient world. (Seriously, for a founder of a clan known for its use of Obfuscate, Set sure gets a lot of stories told about him. Maybe it's more appropriate if his worshippers have just been inserting myth and legend to fill the gaps in what they know about where their blood comes from.)</div> </div> </div> </div>The Ministry in Players Guide make it sound like that they may have taken the name of Set to use as a cover for there activities and I don't really mind the possible sub plot as they are a clan of lies.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-31T09:49:23-07:00","id":1536626,"title":"","content":"Having been reading about Roads and Paths for DAV20 recently, I came across something that made me consider some interesting possibilities. DAV20 has a split between the Road of Set, which focuses on devotion to Set's religion and followers, and the semi-heretical Path of Apep, which focuses more on corruption and chaos as good for their own sake. I'm less familiar with Setite lore than I am with the background for some other Clans, but I do recall it being mentioned somewhere that many older Setites (pre-Christian Setites in particular) see the whole snake thing as new, maybe even a corruption of their original beliefs.<br /> <br /> I think it opens up an interesting possibility for Set and his Followers to have an almost tragic backstory. It could be that Set originally was more of a righteous protector who genuinely acted for what he thought to be the good of everyone, but either by choice or misfortune ended up being corrupted by Apep (Oblivion/the Wyrm/????). Slowly, over time, the corruption within spreads, until hedonism and depravity become the defining feature of the Clan/faith, and its more liberating and idealistic aspects are pushed to the fringes.<br /> <br /> Always appreciate threads like this, helping me get insight into Clans I have given less attention to over the years <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-31T10:43:08-07:00","id":1536630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kharnov</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1536626#post1536626\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having been reading about Roads and Paths for DAV20 recently, I came across something that made me consider some interesting possibilities. DAV20 has a split between the Road of Set, which focuses on devotion to Set's religion and followers, and the semi-heretical Path of Apep, which focuses more on corruption and chaos as good for their own sake. I'm less familiar with Setite lore than I am with the background for some other Clans, but I do recall it being mentioned somewhere that many older Setites (pre-Christian Setites in particular) see the whole snake thing as new, maybe even a corruption of their original beliefs.<br /> <br /> I think it opens up an interesting possibility for Set and his Followers to have an almost tragic backstory. It could be that Set originally was more of a righteous protector who genuinely acted for what he thought to be the good of everyone, but either by choice or misfortune ended up being corrupted by Apep (Oblivion/the Wyrm/????). Slowly, over time, the corruption within spreads, until hedonism and depravity become the defining feature of the Clan/faith, and its more liberating and idealistic aspects are pushed to the fringes.<br /> <br /> Always appreciate threads like this, helping me get insight into Clans I have given less attention to over the years <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>re: Set as a positive figure, later corrupted: that's indeed one of the possibilities given by the lore in various editions, and it's pointed to in the Setite apocrypha from VDA20 Companion, as well as that from the core manual, of course.<br /> <br /> As for the snake aspect, VDA20 tends to give the impression that it is more ancient, but in a complicated way, and one should look at the Tome of Secrets as well (the section on Setite Sorcery) to get a better idea. But the core manual, when introducing the Warriors of Glycon and the Witches of Echidna, does make reference to the Gifts of Typhon and Echidna as the older stuff, and they're both snake-y beings. Of course, one could say that these two bloodlines are the new deviants, but that's not said nor, apparently, implied. Rather, they are eliminated by a changed priestly bloodline.<br /> <br /> This is how those sections read to me, at least, and of course nothing has to be &quot;true&quot; when lore is presented in the manuals, as it could be from the biased point of view of particular groups.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-31T12:22:16-07:00","id":1536632,"title":"","content":"This story of an earlier version of Set's cult is actually a reference to the actual cult of Sutekh in ancient Egypt.<br /> <br /> For most of Egyptian history, Set/Sutekh was a good deity. While the myth of his enmity with Osiris already existed in some form, it was just a minor part of his mythology. Set was, among other things, the oldest protector of the Sun against Apep.<br /> <br /> It was only in the &quot;Third Intermediate&quot; era, between the 11th and 7th centuries BC, that occurred a major shift in his religious significance, a process of demonization of him. So his cult had been a positive presence for more than 21 centuries, maybe even more than 5 thousand years.<br /> <br /> Set was linked in Egypt to the &quot;Set animal&quot;, a mysterious quadruped figure usually interpreted in popular culture as a donkey, though historians aren't certain. Only in Greece, after the demonization, and then in Rome, Set became associated with Typhon, and got to be associated with serpents now and then.<br /> <br /> Basically the problem is that the original depiction of the Setites is strongly related to modern pop culture, but has little to no relation to the historical cult (even in Rome the main animal link was the donkey, not serpents, this is a now thing). The reference to a huge change on the cult is an incorporation of actual facts about its past.<br /> <br /> On top of that, while gnosticism is more or less an inspiration all over the place in WoD, it is more directly so for the Cult of Set, indicating that there was some interaction between the Followers and the original Gnostics during early Christianity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-31T13:53:47-07:00","id":1536640,"title":"","content":"I always did have a soft spot for Set in the stories and how he served as a loyal protector for Ra for his voyages to keep the world running.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Power that needs to advertise itself is already losing&quot; Grand Admiral Thrawn <br /> <br /> &quot;Coronation Starscream? This is bad comedy!&quot; Galvatron","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27361-lysander","name":"Lysander","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27361_11.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3523"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-05-31T16:46:07-07:00","id":1536654,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535008-ancient-setite-metaplot-thread?p=1536632#post1536632\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This story of an earlier version of Set's cult is actually a reference to the actual cult of Sutekh in ancient Egypt.<br /> <br /> For most of Egyptian history, Set/Sutekh was a good deity. While the myth of his enmity with Osiris already existed in some form, it was just a minor part of his mythology. Set was, among other things, the oldest protector of the Sun against Apep.<br /> <br /> It was only in the &quot;Third Intermediate&quot; era, between the 11th and 7th centuries BC, that occurred a major shift in his religious significance, a process of demonization of him. So his cult had been a positive presence for more than 21 centuries, maybe even more than 5 thousand years.<br /> <br /> Set was linked in Egypt to the &quot;Set animal&quot;, a mysterious quadruped figure usually interpreted in popular culture as a donkey, though historians aren't certain. Only in Greece, after the demonization, and then in Rome, Set became associated with Typhon, and got to be associated with serpents now and then.<br /> <br /> Basically the problem is that the original depiction of the Setites is strongly related to modern pop culture, but has little to no relation to the historical cult (even in Rome the main animal link was the donkey, not serpents, this is a now thing). The reference to a huge change on the cult is an incorporation of actual facts about its past.<br /> <br /> On top of that, while gnosticism is more or less an inspiration all over the place in WoD, it is more directly so for the Cult of Set, indicating that there was some interaction between the Followers and the original Gnostics during early Christianity.</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point. To be clear, my reading of VDA20's changes to Setite lore, or the way \u0130 would implement them in a chronicle, is that the Setite clan was originally a snake Typhon-and-Echidna (and possibly other mythological beings from Mesopotamian or other mythologies tied to snakes) themed clan, whose Egyptian branch at a later date took on the figure of Set as an addition. And \u0130 don't postulate a Greek origin fir the clan, since the Greek culture known to us is quite young by ancient standards. \u0130 postulate an older origin, and older myths, and cross cultural snake figures (like Tiamat and some of her children in some interpretations), which eventually settled in the Greek form of Typhon and Echidna as far as Mediterranean Cainites are concerned.<br /> <br /> Your reading makes perfect sense in relation to the figure of Set himself, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}